# The frame flow's cadence: THIRTY MINUTES after the last run finished, and a
# first run three minutes after the timer starts (boot, every resume, a release).
#
# THE CADENCE IS THE OWNER'S (2026-09-15). It shipped as one minute (storage-map
# §4d `sync-frame-videos`: "the panel checks EVERY MINUTE"), and on 2026-09-15
# the Owner overrode that on the panel with a drop-in, now retired and carried
# here instead (SR-112): the frame set is a handful of rendered videos that change
# rarely, and a run is not cheap - measured that boot, 92 s of work per 152 s of
# wall clock, so the panel spent ~60% of its life mounting CIFS and walking an
# rsync tree while it was also drawing a UI and playing audio. Thirty minutes
# makes the same run ~5%. It was a DUTY-CYCLE change, not a pile-up fix: runs
# were never overlapping (journal, that day: Failed 14:01:40 -> Starting
# 14:02:41, exactly 61 s idle).
#
# OnUnitInactiveSec, not OnCalendar and NOT OnUnitActiveSec: the period runs from
# the moment the last run FINISHED (DEACTIVATION), so a run that overruns only
# postpones the next one; systemd never starts a second instance of one unit and
# there is no queue to drain. OnUnitActiveSec measures from ACTIVATION, so a run
# longer than the period would re-fire the moment it exited. (The per-flow lock in
# wall-sync.sh is the belt to these braces: wall-sync.service mirrors the frame
# flow too.)
#
# OnActiveSec IS WHAT MAKES IT FIRE AT ALL. OnUnitInactiveSec has nothing to
# measure from until the service has run once: systemd bases it on the service's
# last deactivation (or this timer's last trigger), and a service that has never
# run has neither, so on its own it NEVER arms - the timer sits `elapsed` with
# NextElapseUSecMonotonic=infinity. That is exactly what the panel showed from
# 2026-09-15 to 2026-10-22: no frame-sync run on any boot and no journal entry
# at all (open-items C47). OnActiveSec=3min is measured from THIS TIMER'S OWN
# START, which happens at every boot (timers.target), on every wake
# (wall-sync-resume.service restarts the timer wall-sync-suspend.service stopped)
# and when firstboot restarts it, so it cannot be missed; once it fires, the
# trigger time is the base OnUnitInactiveSec needs. Three minutes keeps the first
# run clear of the boot or resume sync that wall-sync.service is running for the
# same lock. Not OnBootSec: started more than three minutes after boot (every
# resume) it fires at once and races that sync.
#
# ONE COST, MEASURED (systemd 255, 2026-10-22): a `systemctl daemon-reload`
# re-arms OnActiveSec from the moment of the reload, so a burst of reloads (a
# firstboot pass, a release, a package upgrade) is followed by one extra run three
# minutes after the last of them. One run per burst, never a loop; accepted.
# The reverse does NOT happen: a reload never re-arms a timer that is already
# `elapsed` - only a stop/start does, which is why firstboot restarts this timer
# when it finds it elapsed.
#
# THE EMPTY-ASSIGNMENT LESSON, KEPT, AND CORRECTED. The Owner's drop-in said
# `OnUnitInactiveSec=` then `OnUnitInactiveSec=30min`, because these settings are
# LISTS: a drop-in that only sets 30min ADDS a trigger beside the base 1min one,
# the earliest wins, and the cadence does not change (measured: NEXT still 47 s
# away). But the empty assignment resets the WHOLE trigger list - every On*Sec=
# and OnCalendar= alike (systemd.timer(5)) - so it also deleted the base unit's
# OnBootSec=3min, and with it the only trigger that could arm before the first
# run. Reproduced on systemd 255 with both files: TimersMonotonic lists
# OnUnitInactiveUSec=30min alone. Any future drop-in that resets this list must
# restate OnActiveSec; better, change this file, which the release carries.
#
# Persistent= is deliberately ABSENT: it applies to OnCalendar= only, and a
# catch-up run at boot is pointless anyway - wall-sync.service mirrors both flows
# at boot and again on every wake.
[Unit]
Description=Wall panel: check the frame-video share every 30 minutes (OI-18; the Owner's cadence, 2026-09-15)
Documentation=file:///opt/wall-panel/stack/autoinstall/wall/README.md

[Timer]
OnActiveSec=3min
OnUnitInactiveSec=30min
AccuracySec=10s
Unit=wall-sync-frame.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
